WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
It's good to be back.

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:06.560
It’s been five years, doesn’t&nbsp;
look like a lot’s changed. 
 &nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.920
Just two months after walking out of prison,&nbsp;
Martin Shkreli is launching a new business.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.360
His new software platform helps anyone model&nbsp;
chemical compounds to design new drugs for free.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.360
Contributors are paid in the&nbsp;
official crypto “Martin Shkreli Inu”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.720
which has recently crashed&nbsp;
following an alleged hack.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.600
It was his previous involvement with the drug&nbsp;
industry that made the “pharma bro” famous.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:36.160
A few years ago, Shkreli notoriously hiked the&nbsp;
price of a life-saving drug by more than 5000%.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.920
In response to all of this attention,&nbsp;
and doctors and patient groups saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.960
they can’t access this drug, are&nbsp;
you going to change the price?

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.360
No 
 
He was called the “most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:54.104
hated man in America” after raising the price&nbsp;
of a tablet of Daraprim from $13.50 to $750.
 &nbsp;

00:00:54.104 --> 00:00:58.833
I probably would have raised the price&nbsp;
higher is probably what I would have done.

00:00:58.833 --> 00:01:02.000
So how did a guy who started&nbsp;
two hedge funds in his twenties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.280
end up founding Turing Pharmaceuticals?
 
He has a BA in business administration from a city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.400
college in New York yet made the switch because&nbsp;
“...there wasn’t enough money in hedge funds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.920
You could say that that is the biggest dickhead&nbsp;
answer ever” as he admitted to Vanity Fair.
 &nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.880
In 2015, Turing Pharmaceuticals acquired&nbsp;
the rights to Daraprim which treats a rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:28.160
infection from the parasite toxoplasmosis.
 
Babies born to women infected during pregnancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.840
can go blind or suffer brain damage. It can be&nbsp;
fatal in people with compromised immune systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.200
like those living with HIV. 
 
There is no excuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:43.960
for going $13.50 to $750 for one pill. 
 
He looks like a spoiled brat to me. You want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:50.160
to know the truth? He looks like a spoiled brat 
 
“Spoiled brat” is far from the way he grew up. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.400
Shkreli was born in Brooklyn in&nbsp;
1983 to immigrants from Albania. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.680
His father worked as a doorman. Both his parents&nbsp;
also held odd jobs as janitors to support him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.280
his brother, and two sisters. 
 
He’s described himself as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:08.640
most successful Albanian on earth. 
 
Yet many described him differently - targeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.160
him as the arch-villain of the pharma&nbsp;
industry, a symbol of modern-day greed.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.720
What Shkreli did was actually not new.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.760
Drug companies have a history of raising prices. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.400
Yet they didn’t generate the&nbsp;
headlines like Shkreli did. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:28.640
Perhaps because he was so unapologetic about it. 
 
No one wants to say it. No one’s proud of it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:34.720
But this is a capitalist society, capitalist&nbsp;
system, capitalist rules. My investors expect me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.720
to maximize profits, not to minimize them, or go&nbsp;
half, or go 70%, but to go to 100% of the profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.160
curve that we’re all taught in MBA class. 
 
It didn’t help that as people complained that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.680
health care in America was becoming unaffordable,&nbsp;
he portrayed himself as a wealthy hedge-fund guy.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.920
Dishing out $2 million at an auction&nbsp;
to buy the one and only copy of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.680
unreleased Wu-Tang Clan album. 
 
And when Congress grilled him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.840
over the price hike, he trolled them.
 
Do you think you’ve done anything wrong?

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:09.200
On the advice of counsel, I invoke&nbsp;
my fifth amendment privilege&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.480
against self-incrimination and respectfully&nbsp;
decline to answer your question.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.160
Is it pronounced Shkreli?
Yes sir&nbsp;

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.760
See, you can answer some questions,&nbsp;
that one didn’t incriminate you.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:29.200
I truly believe, I truly&nbsp;
believe. Are you listening?

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:32.480
Yes

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.360
He didn’t say much during the hearing, but he’s&nbsp;
defended his actions as having minimal impact on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:42.080
the health care system because Daraprim&nbsp;
is only used by around 2,000 Americans.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:47.040
He said for those without insurance, his&nbsp;
company would sell Daraprim for just $1 a pill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.000
noting more than half of&nbsp;
patients get it for a buck.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.720
For everyone else, insurance&nbsp;
would cover the cost.  
 &nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.320
There isn’t consequences. They have insurance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.440
I don’t know if you’re familiar&nbsp;
with health care insurance. 90%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:01.040
of Americans have it. Our president forced us&nbsp;
to get it. I think. Do you remember that? 
&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.960
Yet healthcare insurance companies claim&nbsp;
they have been forced to raise their rates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:09.520
due to the rising cost of drugs.
 
When you have, for example, health insurers pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.000
really high prices, that gets expressed in&nbsp;
premiums sooner or later. It doesn’t just go away.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.640
Shkreli’s response: Of course it doesn’t. And as&nbsp;
GDPs grow - we’re at the top of the pack - I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.960
what happens is that we learn in our society&nbsp;
is that what we prize in life is our health.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.040
We’re gonna spend more and more on our health&nbsp;
than ever before. And that’s a good thing. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.240
think that’s a mark of a civilized country.  
 
Shkreli claims his price hike was for the greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:39.040
good - that he’d take some of the profit and&nbsp;
put it into research to invent a better drug.
 &nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.760
The least you could do, the least you could do,&nbsp;
is to take those profits and help those patients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.960
even more by making a better drug. Daraprim is 70&nbsp;
years old. Quite frankly, it’s not a great drug.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:54.000
Nevertheless, Daraprim remains the gold&nbsp;
standard for treating toxoplasmosis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.360
Medical professionals are satisfied with its&nbsp;
effectiveness and any side effects are manageable.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:03.760
I’ve reached out to Turing Pharmaceuticals – which&nbsp;
now goes by the name Vyera Pharmaceuticals - to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:08.240
find out how far they’ve come in making&nbsp;
a new drug but have not yet heard back. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:11.280
Its website states it “has&nbsp;
completed early-stage studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.760
to investigate the safety of a novel&nbsp;
molecule” to treat the parasitic disease.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.960
Just a few months after the price hike, Shkreli&nbsp;
resigned as CEO of Turing Pharmaceuticals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:23.920
after being charged with securities fraud.&nbsp;

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:27.840
This had to do with the two hedge funds&nbsp;
he ran before he switched careers.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:32.720
The Securities and Exchange Commission accused&nbsp;
him of lying to investors in his hedge funds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:37.440
about how much money they had and what they&nbsp;
were invested in, and in one instance - lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:42.080
$7 million in a disastrous trade. 
 
To pay back angry investors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.720
prosecutors accused him of ripping off another&nbsp;
drug company that he founded before Turing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.120
called Retrophin (Retro-fin), in effect,&nbsp;
using Retrophin as a personal piggy bank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:55.440
to take millions in stock and cash. 
 
Although the fraud charges were NOT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:00.320
related to the Daraprim case, Shkreli&nbsp;
believes the timing was no coincidence. 
 &nbsp;

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:05.600
Isn’t it ironic that the day they start to charge&nbsp;
is just months after the Daraprim pricing?
 &nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.040
In any case, the criminal charges&nbsp;
didn’t seem to bother him much.&nbsp;

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:13.040
He made faces in court.
He boasted in one of his many live streams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:17.760
that if he got acquitted, he’d sleep with a female&nbsp;
journalist he previously harassed on Twitter which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.680
got him banned from that platform.
Reporter Lauren Duca responded:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:27.200
“I’d rather eat my own organs. So much as touch&nbsp;
me, and I’ll gladly chop off one of yours.”&nbsp;

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:32.080
It appears he thought he’d win the case.
Spirit of Harambe will guide over this trial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:35.680
Justice will be served.
We’re gonna win by a landslide.
 &nbsp;

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:41.920
But that’s not how it played out.
 
On August 4, 2017, a jury found him guilty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:46.800
on three counts of securities fraud.
 
Before sentencing, Shkreli had tears in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:52.320
his eyes as he gave a statement, admitting: “I&nbsp;
was never motivated by money. I wanted to grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:59.120
my stature and my reputation. I am here because of&nbsp;
my gross, stupid, and negligent mistakes I made.”
 &nbsp;

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:04.480
This was a very different Martin Shkreli&nbsp;
than the one the world had come to know. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:09.360
He was free on a five million dollar&nbsp;
bond while awaiting sentencing.&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:14.240
But then…the U.S. District Judge&nbsp;
revoked his bail and threw him in jail.&nbsp;

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.960
Judge Kiyo Matsumoto was not impressed when&nbsp;
he encouraged his Facebook followers to grab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:24.800
strands of Hillary Clinton’s hair during her&nbsp;
book tour, offering to pay $5,000 a strand.&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.280
He said he wanted to use the hair samples to&nbsp;
prove Clinton was a murderer by comparing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:34.000
hair strands to the samples he already had.
In another post, he had offered a bounty to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:38.880
find the person responsible for the murder of&nbsp;
Democratic National Committee staffer Seth Rich.&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.080
Shkreli’s lawyers explained he&nbsp;
was joking but the judge didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:47.600
think it was funny and she revoked his bail.
Shkreli ended up with a seven-year sentence.&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:52.640
He also had to forfeit the $7 million he made from&nbsp;
the fraud to the federal government, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:57.680
giving up his precious Wu-Tang Clan album.
The U.S. Department of Justice sold the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:02.160
album to a group of NFT investors who&nbsp;
reportedly paid $4 million for it.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:08.400
Shkreli was released from prison in May 2022&nbsp;
after serving five years of his sentence.&nbsp;

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:13.680
Prison was weird. It’s not like in the&nbsp;
movies. It’s mostly just guys hanging out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:20.160
playing cards, not doing anything too exciting. 
 
Gonna probably make some babies, repopulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:25.120
America. We’re having a fertility crisis in this&nbsp;
world so it’s important for me to do my job.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:29.920
I think I’m just gonna have like ten or&nbsp;
twenty kids and just Genghis Kahn the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:37.920
Minus the, y’know, the negative&nbsp;
side of that. The consent version.&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:42.240
What else does his future hold,&nbsp;
besides spreading his seed?&nbsp;

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.880
He’s been giving stock advice&nbsp;
on his YouTube channel.&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:48.240
He says he plans to release&nbsp;
his debut album: God’s Gift.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.840
And then there’s his drug&nbsp;
discovery software platform.&nbsp;

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:55.360
He technically can’t work in the&nbsp;
pharmaceutical industry - a federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:59.760
judge banned him for life and it’s not&nbsp;
clear if his new venture violates the ban.&nbsp;

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.320
The Federal Trade Commission and&nbsp;
seven U.S. states had sued Shkreli.

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:08.160
They accused him of tightly controlling&nbsp;
the distribution of Daraprim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:14.080
making it harder for competitors who wanted to&nbsp;
make generics to get samples for required testing.

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:17.040
The patent on Daraprim expired years earlier so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.640
other drug companies could have produced&nbsp;
a generic version to bring down the cost.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:24.960
U.S. District Judge Denise Cote&nbsp;
found Shkreli “violated federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:28.160
and state laws that ban anticompetitive conduct.”
 &nbsp;

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:32.160
She called the Daraprim scheme&nbsp;
“particularly heartless and coercive”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:37.200
and ordered him to pay nearly $65 million&nbsp;
in profits to the states that sued him. 
 &nbsp;

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:40.640
I’m gonna be fighting that ban and winning.&nbsp;
So, I’m going to hand them a nice big fat L&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:45.200
and throw a parade when I win. 
 
As for his former drug company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:50.320
Vyera has agreed to pay $40 million to settle&nbsp;
the government lawsuit over the price hike.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:53.040
It no longer has a monopoly on Daraprim.
 &nbsp;

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:57.680
Starting in 2020, the FDA approved&nbsp;
generic versions of Daraprim.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.400
Instead of $750 a pill, the&nbsp;
non-profit “46Brooklyn” that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:08.000
looks into drug pricing found the generics&nbsp;
are around $300. Which is still expensive.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.280
However, Ben Link with&nbsp;
46brooklyn pointed out to me:

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:16.080
“if the price of the brand never skyrocketed&nbsp;
as much, the generics would likely be cheaper.”

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:20.320
Shkreli said in a recent Reddit&nbsp;
AMA that he still trades,

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:23.760
But he expects this coming&nbsp;
year to be brutal for stocks.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.480
And love him or hate him,&nbsp;
it seems like he’s right.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:30.160
Because the stock market is having&nbsp;
its worst start in 50 years,

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:34.800
And the U.S. economy is on the brink of a&nbsp;
recession, with some saying it’s already here.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:39.520
That’s why it’s important to diversify&nbsp;
your portfolio with alternative assets,

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:42.149
like gold, real estate, and...

00:10:42.149 --> 00:10:44.149
art.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:50.640
That’s why I’m partnering with Masterworks.

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:54.080
Masterworks let’s you invest in&nbsp;
multi-million-dollar paintings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:58.240
by famous artists like Andy&nbsp;
Warhol, Picasso, and Banksy.

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:00.000
Just like buying shares in Tesla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.000
you can buy shares in these artworks&nbsp;
that have been securitized by the SEC.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:08.160
These four paintings Masterworks sold&nbsp;
gave a net annual return of at least 27%.

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:12.720
Even through COVID, a bear&nbsp;
market, and rising inflation.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:17.200
Not to mention art had an average&nbsp;
yearly appreciation of 33%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:21.520
the last time inflation was this
High, according to Masterworks’ All Art Index.

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:26.720
Masterworks is flooded with demand as people are&nbsp;
looking to diversify, so there is a waitlist.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:29.920
But you can skip the waitlist&nbsp;
by clicking my custom link in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:32.480
the description for priority access.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:46.560
Thanks for watching. For Newsthink, I’m Cindy Pom.

